{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0dd98173acfe12970169bb1616bf34819186b9b4287ccb398f", "created": 1773868566, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**CARE TEAM** refers to a multidisciplinary group of clinical, healthcare, and psychosocial professionals and supporting staff who work together to evaluate, diagnose, stage/grade, plan, coordinate, deliver, and monitor a patient’s cancer care (including cancers such as AML, CLL, breast, lung, pancreatic, prostate, bladder, thyroid, head and neck cancers, CSCC, lymphoma, and pediatric cancers). The CARE TEAM gathers and updates key information about the patient over time—medical and family history, symptoms, side effects, lifestyle factors (such as tobacco/alcohol use), quality of life, current medications, vitamins/OTC drugs, herbals/supplements, allergies, and kidney function—and uses this information to guide safe and effective care and avoid harmful drug interactions.\n\nThe CARE TEAM orders, coordinates, and interprets tests and procedures (such as blood/urine tests, imaging, biopsy/pathology review, endoscopy, and bone marrow biopsy), explains what results mean (including cancer subtype, mutations/biomarkers, staging, grade, and risk/prognosis tools such as nomograms), and determines next steps (including whether additional imaging, PSMA/PET scans, or biopsy is needed). In AML care specifically, the CARE TEAM closely monitors blood counts and organ function and guides frequent CBC testing during treatment and recovery.\n\nThe CARE TEAM collaborates with the patient (and caregivers/family when relevant) in shared decision-making to select and adjust a personalized treatment plan based on cancer type/subtype, test results, performance status, treatment tolerance, patient goals and preferences, and how/where the cancer has spread. They explain practical treatment details such as medication choices, dosing, frequency, cycles, potential hospitalization, and follow-up/surveillance plans (for example, deciding ultrasound frequency and interpreting ongoing blood/imaging results in thyroid cancer follow-up). They also assess whether a clinical trial may be appropriate and whether eligibility requirements are met.\n\nThroughout treatment (including chemotherapy, maintenance therapy, radiation, surgery, systemic therapy, and supportive care), the CARE TEAM tracks symptoms and medications, manages side effects and safety risks, provides guidance on preparation instructions for tests (e.g., fasting or medication holds), and helps patients understand what to expect. They address imaging- and procedure-related concerns such as claustrophobia, enclosed MRI anxiety, metal safety, and contrast/tracer use by screening for prior contrast allergies or reactions, weighing whether contrast is appropriate, and prescribing medications or other measures to reduce allergic effects when needed. They also aim to keep patients comfortable during bedside procedures and may arrange sedation or anesthesia when appropriate.\n\nIn addition, the CARE TEAM supports broader health and life concerns, including counseling and referrals (such as fertility preservation and pregnancy planning, contraception discussions, and fertility-sparing options), emotional distress/depression screening and support, nutrition guidance, smoking/vaping cessation resources (including counseling and medications), and coordination of supportive services. The CARE TEAM encourages ongoing communication, questions, and clarification of medical terms/abbreviations, and advises patients to keep CARE TEAM names and contact information accessible and updated, including for urgent questions or emergencies and to report symptoms that could indicate recurrence or relapse.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completio